Do not build AppStream's man pages.

AppStream 1.0.6 includes docs/ unconditionally, and docs/meson.build begins by
running `xsltproc --nonet` against the Docbook manpages stylesheet and calling
`error()` if that returns non-zero. Two reasons that has to go rather than be
satisfied.

The first is that satisfying it means carrying a documentation toolchain for
output nobody reads. It was also, historically, the point where this build first
went wrong: the private prefix was once put on LD_LIBRARY_PATH so each
component's build-time tools could find the libraries just installed, and the
host's xsltproc, which links libxml2.so.2, then loaded the private libxml2 2.13
instead of the system 2.9 it was built against and lost its catalog lookup. That
variable is gone now — every private tool resolves through its own RUNPATH — so
the mismatch no longer occurs, but the dependency on a host xsltproc and a
matching set of Docbook stylesheets would still be a build input nothing pins.

The second is that the output is not wanted. Those man pages document
`appstreamcli`, a tool this runtime does not ship; the shipped tree has no
share/man at all. libadwaita links libappstream for metadata parsing and nothing
else.

Dropping the subdir leaves data/, which still uses itstool, and every library
target untouched. There is nothing to send upstream: this is a local choice not
to build a documentation target, not a defect.

--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -226,7 +226,6 @@
 subdir('po/')
 subdir('data/')
 subdir('contrib/')
-subdir('docs/')
 subdir('tests/')
 if get_option('qt')
     subdir('qt/')
